  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong wire-layer answer to carrier independence: carriers move canonical DAG-CBOR [pcid,payload] bytes, while feed/chunk meaning stays in the pCID-selected protocol. Unknown-pCID opaque relay/store with explicit unsupported-pCID results helps sparse-knowledge transport, but the specimen only partially fits because it does not define feed-level replication or peer-local replication evidence.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear carrier-vs-protocol split via positional [pcid,payload] envelope.",
      "Unknown-pCID policy allows opaque relay/store without speculative parsing.",
      "Canonical bytes and wrapper-based signatures support evolution across carriers."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No feed/chunk protocol, ordering rules, or replication policy.",
      "Auditability drops when pCID handlers are unavailable.",
      "Peer-local evidence/accounting for successful replication is unspecified."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Carrier independence may fail in practice if higher-layer feed protocols leak substrate assumptions.",
      "Long-term interoperability depends on durable pCID/spec discovery without a central registry."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which higher-layer feed protocol fixes chunk identity, ordering, and retry semantics across UDP, git, libp2p, IPFS, and ATPROTO-adjacent carriers?",
      "What local records let Alice or Carol verify replication success when relays only forward opaque envelopes?",
      "How are pCID handlers cached, migrated, and audited over long time spans?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
